      "result": {
        "reply_text": "I understand that you paid for shoes, the seller cancelled the order, and you have not received your refund despite trying to resolve this through customer support.\n\nE-commerce entities are required to have a grievance officer to handle consumer complaints. They must acknowledge your complaint within forty-eight hours and work to resolve it within one month. Additionally, if an e-commerce entity cancels an order unilaterally, they cannot impose cancellation charges on you unless they also bear similar charges themselves.\n\nYou may register your grievance through the National Consumer Helpline (NCH) using the toll-free number 1915, the INGRAM portal, or their mobile app. This is a pre-litigation channel for resolving consumer issues.\n\nIf the issue remains unresolved, you may consider filing a formal complaint with the District Commission. The law allows for complaints to be filed within two years from the date the cause of action arose. The District Commission has the power to order the return of the price paid along with interest.\n\nKeep copies of your invoice, the cancellation email, and your bank statement as proof of your transaction and the seller's failure to refund. You should also keep a record of all your previous communication with the seller's customer support.\n\nIf you are unable to resolve the matter through the National Consumer Helpline or if the seller continues to ignore your requests, you may want to consult a lawyer to discuss filing a formal consumer complaint.\n\nSource: Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020, rule 4\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 39\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 69\n\nWhat I could not verify:\n- Specific refund timelines beyond the general requirement of a reasonable period\n- Whether the seller's specific cancellation policy was agreed upon at the time of purchase\n\nThis is legal information, not legal advice.",
